 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Change delivery over self-hosted WebSockets.** Committed revisions stream to browsers over self-hosted WebSocket sessions, backed by replayable revision storage, reliable publication from the change store, and browser reconnect/catch-up logic. Although the required freshness target is 15 seconds and managed infrastructure is permitted, self-hosted WebSockets was chosen to deliver fast push updates without recurring realtime vendor fees or provider coupling, accepting ownership of connection capacity and reconnect operations. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected despite simpler stateless operation because it adds multi-second lag and repeated cursor reads across up to 800 browsers; a hosted realtime service was rejected despite lower connection-management effort because it introduces recurring fees and couples delivery to a provider's subscription model. See [docs/decisions/change-delivery.md](decisions/change-delivery.md).
